<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    SpotBugs exclusions. Every entry must carry the reason it is here: an exclusion without a
    justification is indistinguishable from a bug someone decided to stop seeing.
-->
<FindBugsFilter xmlns="https://github.com/spotbugs/filter/3.1.0">

    <!--
        Spring configuration properties are mutable by contract: the framework instantiates the
        class and calls the setters. EI_EXPOSE_REP on a String getter is a false positive here,
        and immutable @ConfigurationProperties would break the relaxed binding used elsewhere.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="com.tennisplatform.identity.configuration.IdentityProperties"/>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP,EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        The detector flags any request value reaching a response header and cannot see the
        regex that guards it. The finding was real and was fixed, not silenced: the incoming
        correlation id is now discarded unless it matches [A-Za-z0-9._:-]{1,64}, so control
        characters and overlong values never reach the header or the logs.

        Demonstrated by CorrelationIdFilterTest:
        replacesAnIncomingCorrelationIdThatCouldForgeLogLinesOrSplitTheResponse and
        replacesAnOverlongCorrelationId. Delete this exclusion if that guard ever goes away.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="com.tennisplatform.web.CorrelationIdFilter"/>
        <Bug pattern="HRS_REQUEST_PARAMETER_TO_HTTP_HEADER"/>
    </Match>

    <!--
        The availability services store the outbound ports they are constructed with, which is
        what constructor injection is. There is no internal representation to protect: a
        repository port is a shared collaborator by design, it holds no value of this object,
        and "copying" it is not a thing that can be done.

        It is excluded rather than fixed because there is nothing to fix. What makes it worth
        writing down is that the detector is being inconsistent, not that this module is doing
        something unusual: ManageStudentService, GetTeacherProfileService and
        GetStudentLimitService store their own ports exactly the same way - private final
        fields of an interface type, assigned in a public constructor - and are not flagged.

        Measured rather than assumed, because "the detector is inconsistent" is the kind of
        claim that hides a real defect. A throwaway class in this same package, holding
        AvailabilityRuleRepository, AvailabilityOverrideRepository and teacher's
        GetTeacherProfile side by side in one constructor, was reported on the first two fields
        and not on the third. With the shape of the class held constant, what decides it is the
        type of the field, so there is no arrangement of these services that would satisfy the
        detector - which is what makes an exclusion the honest answer rather than a shortcut.
        Do not widen it to the whole application layer on the strength of that: GetTeacherProfile
        proves the pattern does not flag every port, so a package-wide exclusion would hide more
        than it explains.

        Scoped to these four classes on purpose, so the exclusion cannot quietly cover a real
        EI_EXPOSE_REP elsewhere in the module. A field holding a mutable value - a Date, an
        array, a collection - would still be reported, and would be a genuine finding: see
        AvailabilityView, which copies both of its lists instead of being excluded here.
    -->
    <Match>
        <Class name="~com\.tennisplatform\.availability\.application\.service\.(ConfigureWeeklyAvailability|GetAvailability|ManageAvailabilityExceptions|QueryAvailability)Service"/>
        <Bug pattern="EI_EXPOSE_REP2"/>
    </Match>

</FindBugsFilter>
